The heap window: every low_4gb mapping lies in one window at a base aligned to 4 GiB (ADR-0033 decision 3).

ART keeps a reference to an object as its address in 32 bits and asks MemMap for its heap with low_4gb, which
on arm64 scans [64 KiB, 4 GiB). iOS maps nothing below 4 GiB. At its first low_4gb request, MemMap now reserves a
window without access that ends 4 GiB above a base aligned to 4 GiB, so that the low 32 bits of an address in it
are its offset: all 4 GiB where the system has room for 8 GiB, else only their upper 2 GiB, as without
increased-memory-limit on iOS, where an app gets 7.84 GiB in 4 pieces, the largest 6.00 GiB (spike S2); the upper
half fits into any piece of 6 GiB less a page and leaves 2 GiB more to the rest of the process. The log names the
window once. APKRT_HEAP_WINDOW_UPPER_HALF in the environment, a test's switch, takes the upper half on the Mac too.
Every low_4gb mapping takes a range of the window: at the address it asks for, where that lies in the window and is
free, as mmap treats a hint, else at the top of the highest gap that holds it. So the window fills from the top,
and every object has an offset above 2^31 from the start, where a reference sign-extended somewhere shows at once.
The window's first page stays without access for good, so no object has the offset 0, which is null, and so does
its last page, so no mapping ends at 2^32, whose 32 bits are 0 as well. What MemMap unmaps in the window goes back
to the reservation. How the window is reserved, where a mapping goes and which ranges are mapped is
base/heap_window_ranges.h, a file of the overlay, which runtime/tests/heap_window_test.cpp checks on the Mac.

